on.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339707/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339707"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98758/selection/cd32279b4b3d0706d8e156d1e1c6de45ca61b13223d3c816a4daf17ed60b72c5" :answer/id #uuid "c9971149-1f06-53c6-a99c-ffc724d0f6a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-2$ and $y=3$ are both sinks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98758/selection/503b6460dfd17484ee6c06258f4dde85d67f8438c598968229817915ad8ed751" :answer/id #uuid "8dcde911-bb10-51ba-b5a6-5edfcab188bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a source, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98758/selection/06a87229ff18b850c2f14ecfdda05f1997b9b350ce42cf0eb09f9ed8e53467e6" :answer/id #uuid "cc7003af-528d-5bd0-a31f-dd5aa6ef00d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-2$ and $y=3$ are both nodes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98758/selection/2696461509ead14babc61733ef4c2546126ff6daf2e3b2c63bb2f115d7a28e1a" :answer/id #uuid "8b1ab26b-44fd-5d0a-a2e1-0accb2559e05" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a node" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98758/selection/63388c441f7c07036b47b011d0bba56262144ed1fc089c9a636acb6593b11a3e" :answer/id #uuid "4ed28cf1-eb22-5040-843c-4936d6603f77" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98758/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a88794-7b68-54e4-bcae-0c2c83c98251" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98758/selection/63388c441f7c07036b47b011d0bba56262144ed1fc089c9a636acb6593b11a3e" :answer-field/choices ["q-98758/selection/cd32279b4b3d0706d8e156d1e1c6de45ca61b13223d3c816a4daf17ed60b72c5" "q-98758/selection/503b6460dfd17484ee6c06258f4dde85d67f8438c598968229817915ad8ed751" "q-98758/selection/06a87229ff18b850c2f14ecfdda05f1997b9b350ce42cf0eb09f9ed8e53467e6" "q-98758/selection/2696461509ead14babc61733ef4c2546126ff6daf2e3b2c63bb2f115d7a28e1a" "q-98758/selection/63388c441f7c07036b47b011d0bba56262144ed1fc089c9a636acb6593b11a3e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98758" :question/id #uuid "39f28e41-f7f6-5210-a3c5-5a290e07fa3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98758" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-y-6?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-y-6 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/3d2eec912d188cd569c4f87558a148c808ee2c815eeca7289103f11465d98859.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-2$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-98758/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98758"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98757/selection/a5ff67f25c8f79e9fa6dbc2fe7a6904de42077601537518eb238b06bdf20f4db" :answer/id #uuid "b2712c34-0ab6-5a3a-8a93-2c9c9304db7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a node" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98757/selection/ba7da2ee0a804921c49c3d5e700f3f78b3575386bb64490875d01ff4babe16c1" :answer/id #uuid "94606cbc-892f-5dc1-a035-15608e3b3bc1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=0$ is a sink, and $y=2$ is a source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98757/selection/fa69ce6f539384a065c4a13ca3685c3d23e52e40ede084b17d77c7404cf835c2" :answer/id #uuid "089d3233-051f-5051-a3d6-78c03d1fabbc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=0$ and $y=2$ are both sources" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98757/selection/ef09f9abd062f8914e6317c1b21d06746ac6eef1af4d5c2f1735e80db5485361" :answer/id #uuid "2ec5ac98-4242-53b5-b436-9ea845cc2edf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98757/selection/d0f6fdd9da01e1d36ec5535d994d2449e15a7bad92b06ee8307e0593efba5b1d" :answer/id #uuid "72a42852-2a96-5aae-933f-a20dc4a55746" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=0$ and $y=2$ are both nodes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98757/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6947406-3cc5-5a93-be13-f64945ef97c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98757/selection/ef09f9abd062f8914e6317c1b21d06746ac6eef1af4d5c2f1735e80db5485361" :answer-field/choices ["q-98757/selection/a5ff67f25c8f79e9fa6dbc2fe7a6904de42077601537518eb238b06bdf20f4db" "q-98757/selection/ba7da2ee0a804921c49c3d5e700f3f78b3575386bb64490875d01ff4babe16c1" "q-98757/selection/fa69ce6f539384a065c4a13ca3685c3d23e52e40ede084b17d77c7404cf835c2" "q-98757/selection/ef09f9abd062f8914e6317c1b21d06746ac6eef1af4d5c2f1735e80db5485361" "q-98757/selection/d0f6fdd9da01e1d36ec5535d994d2449e15a7bad92b06ee8307e0593efba5b1d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98757" :question/id #uuid "43ca8274-86e6-5327-bd53-afa9903c842d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98757" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2-y$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and positive for $y\\in (0,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/df68345e3424f1e0020a98ef8dd13456865412818067a822c01c3f50e49386ec.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-98757/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98757"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98763/selection/815b0fea9d897937beeed3b27a73c2b8b877522ec5c01995dbdf46fbb6f1707b" :answer/id #uuid "06f21726-8030-5fc9-a7a9-091cd8b409d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node and $y=3$ is a source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98763/selection/2000131608a17a915f03111d2f3b8e2246b09b30122fa7e647bee4d4b16c2272" :answer/id #uuid "5bcf6f5e-187a-5faf-9b08-2811fdecda6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98763/selection/878dddb083094008480ba9bd43cb5f1cf668db1b5799201394ccf344ec771212" :answer/id #uuid "036a74ed-c94c-585f-816f-3ceff587c392" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98763/selection/74193d619fbf8ce270a128297b509c66bcb1bc7a3f7abeb1a4f87e61d3c9caa1" :answer/id #uuid "01fe33c8-1b70-519a-a364-f3103a3bd5ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98763/selection/42b9332e567a846a9bea627c5bba184afbd0edfa7f91b2ce139d57590b8e1d8e" :answer/id #uuid "c6838c90-d12a-5863-96d3-0456aebfc28c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98763/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c19fbb3d-1027-58c8-9c0d-fef11e4f804c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98763/selection/2000131608a17a915f03111d2f3b8e2246b09b30122fa7e647bee4d4b16c2272" :answer-field/choices ["q-98763/selection/815b0fea9d897937beeed3b27a73c2b8b877522ec5c01995dbdf46fbb6f1707b" "q-98763/selection/2000131608a17a915f03111d2f3b8e2246b09b30122fa7e647bee4d4b16c2272" "q-98763/selection/878dddb083094008480ba9bd43cb5f1cf668db1b5799201394ccf344ec771212" "q-98763/selection/74193d619fbf8ce270a128297b509c66bcb1bc7a3f7abeb1a4f87e61d3c9caa1" "q-98763/selection/42b9332e567a846a9bea627c5bba184afbd0edfa7f91b2ce139d57590b8e1d8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98763" :question/id #uuid "9f6c3551-aac2-5585-bcd3-44729e24f08b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98763" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+6y+9?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+6y+9 & =0 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=-3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $(y+3)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/074ed04c41f20a891678d61a26ae06f1151299110dfd13d2c8b7f6eef3e6e363.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-3$ is a node\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-98763/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98763"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98760/selection/ca6bc447662742edaa012ca9af60c521b9b934ec56c07042ae5beeeb4da71cd1" :answer/id #uuid "7d606246-48cf-5740-bc21-e71d80e0e300" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98760/selection/1611a84050e2f43047ad7dbe0fc31b5984c195851c3c006ccbe8566876e1c55a" :answer/id #uuid "55758d1e-d33b-5677-8be9-3f33f97c7154" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a source, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98760/selection/ebfd2a77a05406a17cc630b241703f556d94ae1dac1588bc81b74f1e23a74b8c" :answer/id #uuid "270a9c82-ba27-54ff-8b05-bf68acf4d9e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a node" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98760/selection/85fee043d9bce069168bb36029df999f0425eaa44fdd2b641e04c499a91485c1" :answer/id #uuid "7a9a6079-f384-506c-8a9c-c8afe0517518" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98760/selection/9e2a6ab16a9ef9e185a5b5d306e3a523fb66c0312fcc67d65077ccb453983781" :answer/id #uuid "42b4755d-8763-5111-ac50-ca89e32e4d8d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98760/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ec143c-b984-58eb-93ce-e72cae8bb019" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98760/selection/85fee043d9bce069168bb36029df999f0425eaa44fdd2b641e04c499a91485c1" :answer-field/choices ["q-98760/selection/ca6bc447662742edaa012ca9af60c521b9b934ec56c07042ae5beeeb4da71cd1" "q-98760/selection/1611a84050e2f43047ad7dbe0fc31b5984c195851c3c006ccbe8566876e1c55a" "q-98760/selection/ebfd2a77a05406a17cc630b241703f556d94ae1dac1588bc81b74f1e23a74b8c" "q-98760/selection/85fee043d9bce069168bb36029df999f0425eaa44fdd2b641e04c499a91485c1" "q-98760/selection/9e2a6ab16a9ef9e185a5b5d306e3a523fb66c0312fcc67d65077ccb453983781"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98760" :question/id #uuid "e1da3364-5c3f-500b-bc7c-7a5f92c432cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-98760" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=-(y-3)({y}^{2}+6y+9)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)({y}^{2}+6y+9) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)(y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-3,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-3$ and $y=3.$\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(y+3)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3),$ and negative for $y\\in (3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/3c118c900486d8561864c88e1b25a461c8f459f810bb7b753d30d9d0d4780913.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-3$  is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-98760/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98760"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-96554/selection/0a32652e2bfa526e7cd4c87dc1bd9d72f34b7c40280aedb3abec1ce50961d65c" :answer/id #uuid "9167b200-5017-5758-9cbc-f4819783322c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=0$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a node" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96554/selection/67d40dc4644f0879ec01b9da29cb6d5c0d349517d48ec2b63cd5fcfaf706b477" :answer/id #uuid "bda47b26-7cb5-539e-bff4-bdcd238b7d43" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=0$ is a source, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96554/selection/210b7a1d9a90aac8c9ec9e6d5fee0b3f1821e5e4cc4a49ac77848e9624896958" :answer/id #uuid "10aee039-ddd8-5bed-8b83-93a4e7b6d8b3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=0$ and $y=3$ are both sources" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96554/selection/58540ec3f1392effa678c6fddfefb9b6de823ef2c417d75fac114350f136ab0e" :answer/id #uuid "470f6110-8460-5960-9b96-03fce0763053" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=0$ and $y=3$ are both nodes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96554/selection/d335ce6c73ebb7c693361091dd7021aa9a41d98d3641ba175248fbaf04fd443c" :answer/id #uuid "b680f59f-01a5-5dc4-a300-f5e189d31cbb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=0$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96554/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4ed55fa-93dd-517c-bb0e-36b8702e4fc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-96554/selection/d335ce6c73ebb7c693361091dd7021aa9a41d98d3641ba175248fbaf04fd443c" :answer-field/choices ["q-96554/selection/0a32652e2bfa526e7cd4c87dc1bd9d72f34b7c40280aedb3abec1ce50961d65c" "q-96554/selection/67d40dc4644f0879ec01b9da29cb6d5c0d349517d48ec2b63cd5fcfaf706b477" "q-96554/selection/210b7a1d9a90aac8c9ec9e6d5fee0b3f1821e5e4cc4a49ac77848e9624896958" "q-96554/selection/58540ec3f1392effa678c6fddfefb9b6de823ef2c417d75fac114350f136ab0e" "q-96554/selection/d335ce6c73ebb7c693361091dd7021aa9a41d98d3641ba175248fbaf04fd443c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-96554" :question/id #uuid "34f74b98-19b4-503e-9cba-984793f00280" :question/math-academy-id "q-96554" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-3y?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-3y & =0 \\\\ y(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (0,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/09fbbcef280dc3d6c0b68e2de23643fae8cbda246863d1b1d325ccb1c6368b4d.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a sink.\n\n- $y=3$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-96554/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-96554"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98774/selection/4b4fec938ccc660cebd176ae93f3ff6ad9556c2f6351b433b7c6f7ec6851cf8e" :answer/id #uuid "6c326e90-6df7-57bc-a33b-babe0a3f367c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/c7895d3f3db254736c4e20ff30dbe92fb7c0786291b1198ed3f727971268be31.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98774/selection/079c399c9f550bcaf23610dae9616904939d6c29639c60a3da515d3e74cdcc56" :answer/id #uuid "18f47973-dc40-5a25-bfe7-4ec27d53d565" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/a375a537b104f5bcdd8f5951277bce2494d8861a7212772a7d7050fa14091cb9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98774/selection/c0cab4fbed4526cf5d28ae09bb867b64fd1affbfd23e05bd66326b13aeed7936" :answer/id #uuid "22ddb781-c3a2-5f6e-a938-f69e1376906c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/31519aa878fd6a9b450296552afd9bbf628e30db68a2d7c6dc6b02a48ae28b06.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98774/selection/60edf91b3edbacf109bc33eefe5a1023da224a9dd006576449b1087c8a620670" :answer/id #uuid "1ccc9153-04b8-5c60-8a77-dbb5e48ff9b0" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/d72110682c1959f10546b6fa49d6e4ebedf6b6d5557639c772f28a920f306df5.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98774/selection/64f0193e75d0035e963bbd8fba671d0bd73835fcae4d155199b7be252cc2e609" :answer/id #uuid "4b513102-c6f7-578d-ad85-e594a933be90" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98774/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "911d25e4-075e-5940-968d-92f845da40f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98774/selection/64f0193e75d0035e963bbd8fba671d0bd73835fcae4d155199b7be252cc2e609" :answer-field/choices ["q-98774/selection/4b4fec938ccc660cebd176ae93f3ff6ad9556c2f6351b433b7c6f7ec6851cf8e" "q-98774/selection/079c399c9f550bcaf23610dae9616904939d6c29639c60a3da515d3e74cdcc56" "q-98774/selection/c0cab4fbed4526cf5d28ae09bb867b64fd1affbfd23e05bd66326b13aeed7936" "q-98774/selection/60edf91b3edbacf109bc33eefe5a1023da224a9dd006576449b1087c8a620670" "q-98774/selection/64f0193e75d0035e963bbd8fba671d0bd73835fcae4d155199b7be252cc2e609"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98774" :question/id #uuid "df1a08a1-49b6-5687-bf86-dc8094436b8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98774" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-4$ (already drawn by the above property) and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/e5ba765b9caffab904f07c5086d3206f72e36e66cb018673ce7ed44d79d19e88.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=-4$ appear to converge toward this line." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-98774/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98774"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-340358/selection/fc7884300d676957f1b425f7ed74db4b6b55522aba8b5eaf4f8c48c7188fcbf5" :answer/id #uuid "3603925f-aba6-55de-afb0-960ba8a8d4a2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/0b8561b868005f359ec3a9d023ada261053fe04e8b3c970fc787a93faff91a5f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340358/selection/724298eb1adb37e2c7a2b5fe930b85e5f4be24630fdc6a53ffc4b3e6991fa640" :answer/id #uuid "a5e2a673-b0fe-5d44-b820-b24e9eef6328" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/ad543fe5e3bda9c57f298c96547e722fea5bed48a4752cac1520b9814b11fa48.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340358/selection/318847850e831d99ba057af85707f8ffa8216122250ddeeeeb40386ca8e6948f" :answer/id #uuid "24865ba0-0d9e-5112-b763-a3c03c64c4d8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/26f65c17a5b108cc0c739bbfa5d7971841bfe71dc8886fb801e2ca5b5d2a32ae.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340358/selection/e89abcb6b679ffa7e467fa32cdff746314ce5b8f211b2a87ab4b0500c2203a23" :answer/id #uuid "034ccd35-2ca3-5b1f-823b-a44152b00c23" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/b0435deaf5351bf3d0c5debe40ef705b8f8b01b6fd4133a2fbca4a12034d9b91.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340358/selection/d85c84312dd9c7df9c5d48fe0e2b9837dac997003d68168827e4e8c340a249ea" :answer/id #uuid "28840676-5d5a-5de0-9fb0-73d2260a78b6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/9295a422a3f393012ebeb664e1b5867483b34e7f35260994364add19a1e99b48.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340358/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d368435-fd4b-5111-ab80-ab1c5d5c91a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-340358/selection/318847850e831d99ba057af85707f8ffa8216122250ddeeeeb40386ca8e6948f" :answer-field/choices ["q-340358/selection/fc7884300d676957f1b425f7ed74db4b6b55522aba8b5eaf4f8c48c7188fcbf5" "q-340358/selection/724298eb1adb37e2c7a2b5fe930b85e5f4be24630fdc6a53ffc4b3e6991fa640" "q-340358/selection/318847850e831d99ba057af85707f8ffa8216122250ddeeeeb40386ca8e6948f" "q-340358/selection/e89abcb6b679ffa7e467fa32cdff746314ce5b8f211b2a87ab4b0500c2203a23" "q-340358/selection/d85c84312dd9c7df9c5d48fe0e2b9837dac997003d68168827e4e8c340a249ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340358" :question/id #uuid "293e0208-526f-5eaf-bcc8-3bedd1343359" :question/math-academy-id "q-340358" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a node\n\n- $y=1$ is a source\n\n- $y=4$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-3,$ $y=1,$ and $y=4.$\n\n- We're given that $y=4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=4$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=1$ (already drawn by the above property) and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-3$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-3$ points downward, the arrow just below it points downward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/e428cf67a486e9eb17764727ab042bb44e07dfe4f13c125112fb56799d5aadea.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that corresponds with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/26f65c17a5b108cc0c739bbfa5d7971841bfe71dc8886fb801e2ca5b5d2a32ae.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3,$ $y=1,$ and $y=4.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=1$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=4$ appear to converge toward this line." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-340358/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697"] :know